# The Weaver of Dreams

In the time before time, when the world was young and formless, there lived a celestial weaver named Somnia. She dwelt in a palace of silver mist, high above the sleeping earth, where she worked at an enormous loom made of starlight and shadow.

Each night, Somnia would gather threads from the day's experiences: golden strands of joy, blue wisps of sorrow, crimson filaments of passion, and grey strings of fear. With nimble fingers, she wove these threads into tapestries of infinite variety—these were the dreams of mortals.

One night, a curious child named Aria refused to sleep, climbing the tallest mountain to find the source of dreams. Somnia, impressed by such determination, revealed herself and offered the child a gift: a single thread from her loom.

"This thread," Somnia said, "connects all dreamers. When you sleep, you may walk through others' dreams and share your own."

Aria accepted gratefully, and from that day forward, storytellers were born—mortals who could weave tales as Somnia wove dreams, connecting hearts and minds across the waking world.

And still, each night, Somnia works her loom, ensuring that every sleeping soul receives their tapestry of dreams.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

